Legacy
in this task the idea is that we have a hash-function, but in old code the size of unsigned was 18 bits, not 32. and hash was getting out of the 
bounds of unsigned faster than in new computers, and since the end of the unsigned was reasched, new values was written in the beggining again 
(like we were starting from first indices again). so to solve this problem we need to apply a mask of 18 number 1 (0b111111111111111111). the mask 
will leave only last 18 symbols, that follows the behaviour of overfilling old unsigned. (on old machine if the value is too big to fit in 18 bits,
everything over 18 bits was ommited by default. thats exactly what we are doing by applying the mask).

unsigned position(const char *s) {
    unsigned h = 0;
    while (*s) {
        h = (h * 67 + *s++) & 0b111111111111111111;
    }
    return h;
}
